WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.480
There's nothing quite as frustrating as a
bad movie ending.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.960
"No!"

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.950
Failure to stick that landing in the final
act can totally ruin an otherwise great movie,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.549
or give us one more reason to hate one that
was already a dud.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:19.510
Here's a look at some of the most annoying
movie endings in recent memory.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.779
And it should go without saying, but...spoilers
ahead…

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:24.400
Matrix Revolutions

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.689
The Matrix trilogy always tried to be more
of a thinking person's action flick than your

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.470
average kung fu kick-fest, so it didn't exactly
come as a surprise when the second and third

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.530
installments took a deeper look at the complex
ideas behind all the action.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.629
Still, it was pretty disappointing when the
series was capped off by a confusing blur

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:47.399
that went from hand-to-hand combat directly
into...poorly explained Eastern mysticism.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.020
"Look look!"

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.460
"Just look at that"

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:56.670
When you end up needing fan sites and message
boards to try to explain the ending of your

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.100
trilogy, you've clearly done something wrong.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.859
X-Men: The Last Stand

00:01:00.859 --> 00:01:05.430
For the third film in the successful X-Men
franchise, screenwriters Simon Kinberg and

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:10.161
Zak Penn tried to bring the force that consumed
and killed Jean Grey, the Dark Phoenix, to

00:01:10.161 --> 00:01:11.640
the big screen.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.090
Unfortunately, budget and studio limitations
prevented the filmmakers from adapting the

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:20.570
comic story properly and fans were left with
a lackluster version of the tale.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:25.250
The restrictions made the deaths of crucial
characters virtually meaningless — and the

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.060
movie's big emotional moment was completely
undercut by a confusing post-credits scene.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.020
"Charles?"

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:40.720
Sure, the movie made money, but The Last Stand
was so widely disliked that it sent the entire

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.920
X-Men franchise into limbo for years.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.000
Signs

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.290
There is no shortage of movies depicting hostile
aliens invading our planet.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.180
But somehow it's a story that needs constant
retelling.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.860
This is likely because it's been quite effective
— and it seems that you've got to really

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.960
go off the rails in order to screw it up.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.330
"There's a monster that's outside my room
can I have a glass of water?"

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.270
Sadly, that's just what M. Night Shyamalan
did with Signs.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:09.360
The film is very well cast and for the most
part tells an effective story.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.700
But unfortunately all of that goes out the
window when this alien thriller revealed that

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.340
the aliens' one big weakness is… friendship
and cuddles.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.570
I'm just kidding.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.130
It was actually just water.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.840
Earth is over seventy percent water.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.840
So isn’t it a little strange that an alien
species advanced enough to travel across the

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:32.200
galaxy and try take over a planet would pick
a destination covered mostly by a substance

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.340
than can destroy them?

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.140
Perhaps Shyamalan will one day reveal that
real twist of Signs was that the aliens were

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.500
on a self destructive intergalactic mission.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.650
That would make a lot more sense.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.490
The Amazing Spider-Man 2

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.140
What do you get when you try to depict one
of the most emotional moments in a comic book

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.560
character's history while also setting up
a slew of studio-mandated spin-offs?

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.380
If you guessed "The Cancellation Of The Third
Movie In The Trilogy," you’re right!

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.990
The last act of The Amazing Spider-Man 2 was
a mess as it tried to squeeze in killing off

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:04.870
Emma Stone's Gwen Stacy right before rushing
into a climax that dealt with the twin dangers

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.110
of Electro and the Green Goblin.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:11.920
As if that wasn’t enough, the movie also
tried to fit in a tease for Sony's then planned

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.080
Sinister Six standalone feature.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.460
Like a lot of subpar superhero movies, it
all ended up being a lot of noise with not

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.920
much emotional impact, and the negative reaction
killed the franchise and sent the quippy webslinger

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.990
swinging right back into Marvel’s welcoming
arms.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.200
Now You See Me

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.160
There's nothing quite as frustrating as a
dumb movie that thinks it's smart.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.540
And Now You See Me is a perfect example of
a confident idiot.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.800
"I am so smart.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.180
I am so smart.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.180
S-M-R-…

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.330
I mean S-M-A-R-T!"

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.320
Films that rely heavily on sleight of hand
and double-crossing are a lot of fun.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.230
But while they're busy with their tricks,
they often forget to tell an actual story

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.180
— and that’s exactly what happened here.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.700
"This is all for show, correct?"

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:59.400
The final act, in which Mark Ruffalo's FBI
agent character reveals himself to be the

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:04.630
shadowy figure behind a secret order of magicians,
is as absurd as it is boring.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:09.230
The most infuriating part of all this is that
the movie has a fantastic cast behind it — and

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:13.341
it's hard not to imagine the angry people
sitting in the theater feeling like the the

00:04:13.341 --> 00:04:14.930
movie had played a trick on them.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:16.220
I Am Legend

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.600
They say the third time's the charm, but that
sadly proved not to be the case for Richard

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.320
Matheson's classic novella I Am Legend.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:28.070
After a pair of poorly made adaptations, Hollywood
had a chance to finally get it right with

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.220
I Am Legend in 2007.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.100
Unfortunately, even after more than 40 years,
Matheson's tale of a scientist living as the

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.350
last man on Earth still turned out to be too
dark for studio execs.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:43.990
The biggest problem: the movie's third act,
in which the main character comes to understand

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:47.911
that instead of being humanity's final hope,
he's actually a monster in the eyes of the

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.160
planet's new civilization.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.160
"Let me save you!

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.440
Let me save you!"

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.680
After test screening audiences rejected the
original ending — you know, the one that

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:05.720
was in the actual book it was based on — director
Francis Lawrence had to reshoot it, thus turning

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.660
a potentially thought-provoking picture into
just another forgettable CGI Editor’s demo

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.210
reel.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:13.210
Man of Steel

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.360
Some people hate it, some people love it.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.151
But regardless of where you stand, there's
no denying that Man of Steel has one of the

00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:22.500
most hotly debated movie endings in recent
memory.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.680
Some fans were on board with director Zack
Snyder's over-the-top climactic battle, which

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.490
ends with Superman snapping General Zod's
neck.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:34.199
But many moviegoers who grew up with Superman
felt that his decision to execute the movie's

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:37.540
villain was unnecessary — and even downright
offensive.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:42.020
Man Of Steel’s ending also received serious
backlash for the amount of destruction and

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.690
death it brought flying through the core of
Metropolis.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:49.010
The giant box office numbers might suggest
that fans loved this movie...but just about

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:53.570
any film about Superman is going to make money,
so maybe that’s not the best measuring stick.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.949
Although it wasn’t as critically panned
as Batman V Superman,

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:00.190
"Hello darkness my old friend."

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:04.669
Man Of Steel definitely presented an ending
that had many fans up in arms.

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:06.940
Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:11.510
Generally speaking, there's nothing wrong
with the way the Lord of the Rings saga concludes.

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:16.550
Frodo completes his quest, the ring gets all
melted, and everyone lives happily ever after.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:20.591
Yet, it seemed like director Peter Jackson
really didn’t want the movie to end, as

00:06:20.591 --> 00:06:25.700
the conclusion seems to drag on and on before
finally cutting to the end credits.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:29.520
When you look at the series of scenes that
slowly plays out over the last act of The

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.010
Return of the King, it seems like the movie
just doesn’t want to, like, go away.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:38.090
And just when you think the movie is finally
over, you find yourself sitting through yet

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:41.450
another leisurely look at life after the fall
of Sauron.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:45.670
Instead of feeling satisfied at the end of
a story well-told, lots of fans found themselves

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:49.190
getting bored while a bunch of Hobbits jumped
around on a bed in slow motion.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.930
Damn those slow motion hobbits!

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:52.940
"Haha."

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:55.960
Thanks for watching!

